<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Anthem · c. 850 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ave maris stella</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A medieval hymn that calls Mary the &quot;star of the sea&quot;, sung by travellers and adopted as the anthem of the Acadians.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous; attributed at various times to Venantius Fortunatus, Hermannus Contractus and Bernard of Clairvaux</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (plainchant)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Latin Christendom; later the Acadians</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>probably 9th century; added in the 10th century to two 9th-century manuscripts</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous Latin hymn, probably of the 9th century)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ave-maris-stella">Ave maris stella</a>&quot; (&quot;Hail, star of the sea&quot;) is a medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> hymn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>, usually sung at Vespers. Its author is unknown: it has been attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venantius-fortunatus">Venantius Fortunatus</a> in the 6th century, Hermannus Contractus in the 11th and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a> in the 12th. It probably originated in the 9th century. It appears as a 10th-century addition in two 9th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, one from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, and one still kept at the Abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Saint Gall</a>.</p><p>Sung often in the Divine Office, it was especially popular in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and other hymns were founded upon it. It did much to present Mary as a merciful and loving mother. Its title, &quot;Star of the Sea&quot;, is one of the oldest and most widespread titles given to Mary, and the hymn is often used as a prayer for the safe conduct of travellers. Its melody is also found in the Irish plainsong &quot;Gabhaim Molta Bríde&quot;, a song in praise of Saint Brigid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a>, and the modern hymn &quot;Hail Queen of Heaven, the Ocean Star&quot; is loosely based on that plainsong.</p><p>The Roman Rite uses four plainchant tunes for the hymn, three for the feasts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> and a fourth in the Little Office of the Blessed Virgin Mary. Composers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> to the present have built on it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josquin-des-prez">Josquin</a> and Victoria used its tunes as the cantus firmus of Masses; Dufay, Palestrina, Byrd and others set it in the Renaissance; Monteverdi included it in his Vespers of 1610, and the Emperor Leopold I, Louis Couperin (1658) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marc-antoine-charpentier">Marc-Antoine Charpentier</a> (four settings, 1670s–1680s) set it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonin-dvorak">Dvořák</a>, Grieg, Liszt and Elgar in the 19th century; and Marcel Dupré, Peter Maxwell Davies, James MacMillan and others in modern times.</p><p>In 1884 the second Acadian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a>, meeting at Miscouche on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-edward-island">Prince Edward Island</a>, adopted &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ave-maris-stella">Ave maris stella</a>&quot; as the anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of greeting and prayer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> under her old title of star of the sea, presenting her as a merciful and loving mother to whom travellers turn for a safe journey. The mood is gentle, simple and trusting.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Ave, maris stella,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hail, star of the sea,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Dei mater alma,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>nurturing mother of God,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">atque semper virgo,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and ever virgin,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Felix cæli porta.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>happy gate of heaven.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Sumens illud Ave</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Taking that &quot;Ave&quot;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Gabrielis ore,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from the mouth of Gabriel,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">funda nos in pace,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>ground us in peace,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">mutans Evæ nomen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>turning around the name of Eva.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Solve vincla reis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Loose the chains of the guilty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">profer lumen cæcis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>bring out light for the blind,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">mala nostra pelle,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>drive away our evils,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">bona cuncta posce.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>ask for all good things.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Monstra te esse matrem,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Show yourself to be a mother:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">sumat per te precem</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>may he take up our prayer through you</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">qui pro nobis natus</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who, born for us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">tulit esse tuus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>chose to be yours.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Virgo singularis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Virgin without equal,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">inter omnes mitis,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>gentle above all others,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">nos culpis solutos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make us, loosed from our faults,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">mites fac et castos.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>gentle and chaste.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Vitam præsta puram,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Grant a pure life,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">iter para tutum,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make ready a safe road,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ut videntes Jesum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>so that, seeing Jesus,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">semper collætemur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we may rejoice together for ever.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Sit laus Deo Patri,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Praise be to God the Father,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">summo Christo decus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>glory to Christ most high,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Spiritui Sancto</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to the Holy Spirit:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">tribus honor unus. Amen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to the three one honour. Amen.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous Latin hymn; as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ave_maris_stella" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>line 2.4: 'mutans Evae nomen': 'Ave' is 'Eva' (Eve) read backwards, Mary undoing Eve's fall.</li><li>line 4.2: 'precem' has no 'our' in the Latin: 'the prayer'.</li><li>line 4.4: 'tulit esse tuus', literally 'bore to be yours', i.e. took on being your son.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="8TGW9k4zxhY" data-title="Ave maris stella: Gregorian chant"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Gregorian chant</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Verbum Gloriae. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8TGW9k4zxhY" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gabhaim Molta Bríde</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (Irish plainsong in praise of Saint Brigid)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hail Queen of Heaven, the Ocean Star</span><span class="sg-chain-h">loosely based on the plainsong</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Masses (Josquin des Prez; Tomás Luis de Victoria)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">its tunes used as cantus firmus</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Settings (Hans Leo Hassler, Felice Anerio, Palestrina, Guillaume Dufay, John Sheppard, William Byrd)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Vespro della Beata Vergine (Claudio Monteverdi)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1610</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Setting (Emperor Leopold I)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Setting (Louis Couperin)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1658</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Four settings, H.60, H.63, H.65, H.67 (Marc-Antoine Charpentier)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1670s–1680s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Setting (Sébastien de Brossard)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Settings (Antonín Dvořák, Edvard Grieg, Franz Liszt, Edward Elgar)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Settings or works inspired by it (Marcel Dupré, Flor Peeters, Abel Decaux, Peter Maxwell Davies, Grace Williams, James MacMillan, Jean Langlais, Cecilia McDowall)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">settings or used as inspiration</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Anthem of Acadia</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1884</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adopted as a national anthem</span></li></ul></section>
